WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.069
- The fake McDreamy's back, AKA me.

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:06.090
I'm gonna be watching my second
episode of Grey's Anatomy

00:00:06.090 --> 00:00:07.490
alongside you guys.

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:08.561
Let's get it started.

00:00:08.561 --> 00:00:09.394
Bee, whoop!

00:00:09.394 --> 00:00:11.977
(lively music)

00:00:16.058 --> 00:00:17.930
(static hisses)
- [Man] Turn up the TV.

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.300
- [Reporter] A massive
train wreck occurred

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.159
just outside of Seattle minutes ago.

00:00:21.159 --> 00:00:23.893
- 911? We just worked a 30 hour shift.

00:00:23.893 --> 00:00:27.000
- I don't have any clean underwear.

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:27.977
- [Reporter] The Vancouver-bound
train was carrying...

00:00:27.977 --> 00:00:30.212
- That's crazy, they
worked a 30-hour shift

00:00:30.212 --> 00:00:31.900
and they can get paged to go back?

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:35.730
- Hello. I seem to be a little bit drunk.

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:36.910
I was off duty.

00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:38.140
- So was I.

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:39.620
Anybody else half in the bottle?

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:40.453
- [Together] No.

00:00:40.453 --> 00:00:41.286
- I don't get why she would

00:00:41.286 --> 00:00:44.320
come in to her shift if she's drunk.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:46.690
That's not helpful to anybody,

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:50.350
and if she starts performing
any kind of medical procedures

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.840
while under the influence,

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.680
that's not good for patients,
that's not good for her,

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.410
it's just a subpar solution,

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:58.570
so I don't really understand
what she's even doing there.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:02.513
She should be making a
little love with McDreamy.

00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:07.430
- Was that a nod?

00:01:08.309 --> 00:01:09.800
- Yes.
- Do we know what it meant?

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:10.633
- No.

00:01:13.101 --> 00:01:13.934
- Am I invisible?

00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:15.040
- How much romance?

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:16.970
I feel like this isn't
a medical show anymore,

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.238
it's just a romance show.

00:01:18.238 --> 00:01:19.900
(dramatic music)

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:20.733
Whoa.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.913
What is that?

00:01:26.070 --> 00:01:28.200
That.
- [Meredith] Okay, fine.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:29.390
Maybe it's the second worst.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:30.680
That was crazy.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:34.430
- So are you gonna pull this
pole out of us anytime soon?

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:35.794
- Touch uncomfortable.

00:01:35.794 --> 00:01:36.627
(laughing)

00:01:36.627 --> 00:01:38.380
- I'm sorry, we can't do that
until we get a better look

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:39.560
at what's going on internally.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.510
But I assure you, we will
work as quickly as possible.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:45.760
- They're taking this very not-seriously

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.200
and a lot of humor with a pole

00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:50.930
jammed into two peoples' bodies.

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:53.660
Whoa, that's all I can
really say right now.

00:01:53.660 --> 00:01:55.900
Something that I think
needs to be done immediately

00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:58.040
is to give them some sort of back support

00:01:58.040 --> 00:02:00.410
so that they don't get fatigued and tired

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.860
and start slouching, 'cause
if they are to slouch

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:05.400
when they get tired, and they
obviously will get tired,

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.750
they can do more internal damage

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:07.920
with that pole going through them.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.562
- You are drunk.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:11.550
Go get yourself a banana bag IV,

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.210
put it in your arm and then find me.

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:15.110
Do not speak to any more patients,

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.844
do not practice any medicine.

00:02:16.844 --> 00:02:19.990
- IV drips do not stop hangovers.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:23.590
This is why so many people are
opening these IV drip spas,

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:25.640
especially in Vegas I
see that happen a lot,

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:26.750
and they promise you that it's gonna

00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:28.270
block or cure your hangover.

00:02:28.270 --> 00:02:32.230
An IV will hydrate you the same

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.320
that you can hydrate yourself
by drinking some water

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:37.670
or drinking some fluids
with electrolytes in them.

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:39.830
Now that being said, it's
gonna be a little bit quicker,

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.020
but then again you're
also increasing risks

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:45.610
'cause you're opening a hole
in your skin to get that IV in.

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:47.280
Something to mention about hangovers

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:49.780
is that it's not all about dehydration.

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:51.740
A hangover is like a catchall term

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:52.780
for all sorts of symptoms.

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.670
Nausea, vomiting, fatigue,
dizziness, a dry mouth.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:02:59.780
Yes, hydration may help
some of these things,

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:01.210
but it's a complicated process

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:02.830
that has a lot of things going on in it.

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:05.630
Just giving yourself hydration
is not the sole answer.

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:07.690
- No, no, no, we're fine honey, yeah.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.680
Some redneck tried to outrun the train.

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.590
Honey, the train slammed into his ass

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:12.530
and then it rolled.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:13.970
- [Alex] That hurt?
- [Patient] Mm-mm.

00:03:13.970 --> 00:03:15.190
- [Yvonne] Oh, no, no, no, no, no.

00:03:15.190 --> 00:03:16.260
That dude is toast.

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:19.410
- He was doing sutures, it
looked like, above her eyebrow.

00:03:19.410 --> 00:03:21.610
No doctor would perform sutures like that.

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:24.050
Keeping your hands elevated like that

00:03:24.050 --> 00:03:26.260
from a lowered position and doing sutures

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:28.410
you're gonna get fatigued so quickly,

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:29.850
and your hands are gonna start shaking,

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:31.110
and you won't be able to do it right.

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.570
You always need to be
above the given situation.

00:03:33.570 --> 00:03:34.490
- [Yvonne] Where you going?

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.143
- I'm taking your friend for an X-Ray.

00:03:36.143 --> 00:03:37.170
- Oh. (laughs)

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.570
No offense little boy, but, uh,

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.300
you look like my oldest son
and he's nothing but trouble.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:42.520
- Yvonne, shut up.

00:03:42.520 --> 00:03:44.890
- What, I said no
offense, I'm just saying.

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:46.031
Are you sure you're a doctor?

00:03:46.031 --> 00:03:47.510
(playful ringtone rings)

00:03:47.510 --> 00:03:48.490
- I get that all the time.

00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:50.340
- [Izzy] What are you doing?

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:52.450
- Trying to insert my banana bag.

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:54.340
Which sounds vaguely dirty, but it isn't.

00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.780
- A banana bag is just an
IV bag with normal saline,

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:59.630
which is fluids with electrolytes in them.

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.080
There's also some vitamins in there.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:02.520
Generally, this is given to people

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:04.150
who are chronic alcoholics,

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.410
'cause they're usually nutrient depleted.

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:08.270
Giving a banana bag to someone

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:10.290
who's healthy and had a few drinks

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:11.620
pretty much useless.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:13.480
Just have some water
and give it some time.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:14.690
- [Cristina] His legs.

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:15.603
- What about them?

00:04:18.130 --> 00:04:19.253
- They're both left.

00:04:21.608 --> 00:04:23.980
(playful music)

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.270
- Find the man's leg, Yang.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:27.883
Find it now.
- [Cristina] Yes, sir.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:29.757
- They got the wrong leg?

00:04:29.757 --> 00:04:31.340
- Is it going straight through her spine?

00:04:31.340 --> 00:04:33.373
And why did she say it only then?

00:04:34.390 --> 00:04:35.550
- [Derek] These people are still alive?

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.630
- They're still making smalltalk.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.470
- Pole's tamponading the
wound as far as we can see.

00:04:40.470 --> 00:04:41.550
- When she says that the wound

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:44.670
is being tamponaded by the pole,

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.690
it means that it's exerting pressure

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:49.230
and actually preventing
them from bleeding.

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:51.210
That's why if you ever get
stabbed with something,

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.870
it's never ideal to just
pull it out automatically

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:55.430
until you have medical care.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:57.110
That object, whatever it is,

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:00.000
is putting pressure to stop the bleeding.

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:01.360
- What if we move one of the patients

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.273
off the pole to get the saw in there?

00:05:04.625 --> 00:05:07.263
Then we can hold the pole
steady in the other one.

00:05:07.263 --> 00:05:10.540
Move it very slowly and
repair the damage as we go.

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:13.363
- Who? Which would you move?

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:18.170
- With her aortic injuries,
her chances of survival

00:05:18.170 --> 00:05:19.900
are extremely slim no matter what we do.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.920
- I don't really buy the realism of this.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:25.060
The aorta is a pulsatile vessel,

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:28.070
meaning that every time the
heart beats, the aorta moves.

00:05:28.070 --> 00:05:31.510
So if you have a metal pole
that's going through the aorta

00:05:31.510 --> 00:05:32.840
and has cut it open,

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:35.480
there is no way that pole is
putting that much pressure

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.710
that it's preventing her from bleeding out

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.740
unless it's crushing it or compressing it

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:40.990
in such a weird way.

00:05:40.990 --> 00:05:43.740
- So basically whoever you
move doesn't stand a chance.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:47.610
- Why can't they just cut
the pole from in between them

00:05:47.610 --> 00:05:48.443
and then separate them

00:05:48.443 --> 00:05:50.440
and go...
- So how do you choose?

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:52.750
How do you decide who gets to live?

00:05:52.750 --> 00:05:54.360
- You're a cute doctor.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:56.743
Cute doctors get to call
me by my first name.

00:05:58.760 --> 00:05:59.971
- Bonnie.

00:05:59.971 --> 00:06:00.804
- Mm.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.490
- Okay, Bonnie.
- Oh, McDreamy.

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:05.700
Can you feel me rubbing your foot?

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:08.163
- Could you try to wiggle
your toes, Mr. Maynard?

00:06:10.230 --> 00:06:11.530
- Are they moving?

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:12.363
- Yes they are.

00:06:12.363 --> 00:06:14.030
- Huh, good. (laughs)

00:06:14.030 --> 00:06:15.079
That's good, right?

00:06:15.079 --> 00:06:16.370
- Yes it is, yes it is.

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:18.103
- What about me, are mine moving?

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:22.923
- Yes they are.

00:06:22.923 --> 00:06:25.303
- (sighs) Yay, me.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.080
- That's probably one of the situations

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:29.440
it's okay to lie to a patient.

00:06:29.440 --> 00:06:31.020
- Is there any chance that we can

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:33.009
get out of here sometime this year?

00:06:33.009 --> 00:06:35.190
- Well, the lab's backed
up and so is radiology

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:36.850
and I can't discharge your
friend until I'm certain

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:38.029
she doesn't have any internal injuries.

00:06:38.029 --> 00:06:39.981
(playful ringtone rings)

00:06:39.981 --> 00:06:42.960
Nowadays, we decide if
someone has internal bleeding

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:44.810
with an ultrasound called a FAST scan.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:46.020
- If I don't find this leg,

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:47.617
the chief is gonna cut
me from this program

00:06:47.617 --> 00:06:49.820
and I cannot go back home, Burke.

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:51.190
It is too sunny on Los Angeles.

00:06:51.190 --> 00:06:52.420
It's sunny everyday.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.080
- I don't understand what she expects.

00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:56.330
Does she think that the
chief is gonna be like,

00:06:56.330 --> 00:06:58.530
you couldn't find this leg in the rubble

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.050
so that's it, you're cut from the program?

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:01.420
I don't understand what her thinking is.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:03.610
Is this supposed to be
like a comedic aspect?

00:07:03.610 --> 00:07:04.740
Is she being sarcastic?

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:06.680
- In order to operate on Mr. Maynard,

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.080
we have to separate you two.

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:12.590
In order to do that, we have to move you

00:07:12.590 --> 00:07:13.972
backwards off the pole.

00:07:13.972 --> 00:07:16.419
(sad music)

00:07:16.419 --> 00:07:18.560
- Can't just pull the
pole out of both of us?

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:19.660
- Well if we did that,

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.320
you would both start
bleeding very quickly.

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:23.850
Too quickly.

00:07:23.850 --> 00:07:26.170
Right now, the pole is
plugging the wounds.

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:29.223
Once removed, the organs will shift.

00:07:30.750 --> 00:07:32.550
And there is a great deal of damage.

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:38.883
- So, if you move me, I'll die?

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:43.640
- You're being ridiculous.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:45.090
You're gonna be fine, do you hear me?

00:07:45.090 --> 00:07:45.923
- I hear you.

00:07:45.923 --> 00:07:48.070
But we still have to sign the forms.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:49.832
Unless you want our son
to end up with my mom.

00:07:49.832 --> 00:07:52.437
(playful music)

00:07:52.437 --> 00:07:54.687
(laughing)

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:57.930
- How long have you two been together?

00:07:57.930 --> 00:07:59.417
- Since third grade
- Aww.

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:02.090
- We're not lovers,

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:03.540
we're best friends.
- Oh.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.990
- We just wanted our
kids to have two parents.

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:07.283
So we got a sperm donor.

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:09.710
- The same donor.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:11.620
So your babies are brother and sister?

00:08:11.620 --> 00:08:13.387
- Oh, how cool is that?

00:08:13.387 --> 00:08:15.022
- (laughs) That's, wow.
- Oh my god. (laughs)

00:08:15.022 --> 00:08:16.859
God, who writes this stuff?

00:08:16.859 --> 00:08:18.513
(playful ringtone rings)

00:08:18.513 --> 00:08:19.580
(papers shuffle)

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:20.980
- Yvonne, answer your phone.

00:08:22.500 --> 00:08:24.263
Yvonne, Yvonne.

00:08:25.363 --> 00:08:26.196
(Yvonne thuds)
(tools clatter)

00:08:26.196 --> 00:08:28.160
Yvonne!
- Uh oh, Yvonne looks like

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:29.095
she passed out.
- Code Blue!

00:08:29.095 --> 00:08:30.030
- We need some help over here!

00:08:30.030 --> 00:08:32.230
- Okay, first thing you
do during a Code Blue:

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:34.350
chest compressions, chest
compressions, chest compressions,

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:35.710
chest compressions.

00:08:35.710 --> 00:08:38.610
But also, before you call a Code Blue,

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:39.760
you gotta check for a pulse.

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:41.820
'Cause Code Blue means
someone's heart has stopped.

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:43.660
And just because someone's unconscious,

00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:45.120
it doesn't mean they're a Code Blue.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:48.100
So my man needed to go right
up there to the carotid,

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:50.630
or to the femoral artery,
or to the radial artery,

00:08:50.630 --> 00:08:52.090
this is usually the best place,

00:08:52.090 --> 00:08:53.400
and feel if there's a pulse.

00:08:53.400 --> 00:08:55.150
If there's no pulse, yelling for help

00:08:55.150 --> 00:08:57.300
while doing chest compressions.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:08:59.740
- She's lost too much blood,
she's been bleeding for hours.

00:08:59.740 --> 00:09:01.120
- There's no blood, she's not bleeding.

00:09:01.120 --> 00:09:02.350
- She was bleeding internally.

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:04.610
- What's interesting
about the abdominal cavity

00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:07.510
is that the back portion,
the posterior portion,

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:09.610
which we call the retroperitoneal area,

00:09:09.610 --> 00:09:12.750
it can actually store a lot of blood.

00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:14.410
So you can be having a very slow

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:16.080
leak of blood into that area,

00:09:16.080 --> 00:09:18.230
and not really know that
you're bleeding out.

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:19.920
The one thing that makes this less likely

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.420
is that she was talking,
she was moving around.

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:23.770
Generally, if you're losing blood

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:26.080
you start having symptoms
like you feel fatigued,

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:27.130
your eyes are closing,

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:29.380
you feel like you're
gonna faint or pass out,

00:09:29.380 --> 00:09:31.480
your blood pressure starts dropping,

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:33.800
you could even be having
some abdominal pain,

00:09:33.800 --> 00:09:37.500
'cause blood is a natural
irritant to the abdominal cavity

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.610
so you start feeling pain,
and then once we do our exam

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:42.560
we'll note that you have an acute abdomen.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:44.390
We would do an ultrasound or a CAT scan,

00:09:44.390 --> 00:09:46.280
find the bleeding, and then take you in

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:48.640
for what we call an
explorative laparotomy,

00:09:48.640 --> 00:09:51.020
where we literally open up your stomach,

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:53.190
by stomach I mean the
skin of your abdomen,

00:09:53.190 --> 00:09:55.590
look around, check the entire intestines,

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:57.900
find where you're bleeding, fix that area,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:59.990
put everything back in, sew you back up,

00:09:59.990 --> 00:10:01.860
and get you up and moving again.

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:03.870
But in this case, she's lost so much blood

00:10:03.870 --> 00:10:05.500
that she went into cardiac arrest,

00:10:05.500 --> 00:10:06.760
meaning her heart stopped.

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:10.410
Now he's doing the advanced
cardiac life support procedure

00:10:10.410 --> 00:10:12.080
where he's giving epinephrine,

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:13.620
he's doing chest compressions,

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:16.820
he's helping her breathe with the bag,

00:10:16.820 --> 00:10:18.480
and hopefully trying to get her pulse back

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:19.528
and get her heart beating again.

00:10:19.528 --> 00:10:21.883
- No, no, no, Yvonne!

00:10:21.883 --> 00:10:22.883
- I'm sorry.

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:26.880
Time of death, 2:51.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.940
(dramatic music)

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:31.190
- [Nurse] Watch her head.

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:35.183
- [Nurse] Clean towel.

00:10:37.092 --> 00:10:39.425
(saw whirs)

00:10:40.350 --> 00:10:41.690
- [George] Meredith.

00:10:41.690 --> 00:10:43.460
- [Bailey] Doctor Grey.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:44.304
- [George] Meredith.

00:10:44.304 --> 00:10:45.379
- What about her?

00:10:45.379 --> 00:10:48.726
We cannot just abandon
her, we have an obligation.

00:10:48.726 --> 00:10:50.809
- I'll take care of this.

00:10:51.901 --> 00:10:52.750
Meredith, come on, come on.

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:53.810
There was too much damage.

00:10:53.810 --> 00:10:55.440
There was never anything we could do.

00:10:55.440 --> 00:10:56.723
We have to let her go.

00:11:01.300 --> 00:11:02.870
Time of death, 3:49.

00:11:02.870 --> 00:11:05.780
- One of the most difficult
parts about medicine is triage.

00:11:05.780 --> 00:11:08.500
Knowing who you can help in a given moment

00:11:08.500 --> 00:11:11.130
and knowing where to use resources

00:11:11.130 --> 00:11:12.310
when they're very limited.

00:11:12.310 --> 00:11:15.380
Some of those decisions
are heart-wrenching,

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:17.640
they're incredibly difficult to make.

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:19.650
At times, it feels like you're playing God

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:23.370
and it's putting you in
the most unfair situations.

00:11:23.370 --> 00:11:24.980
But you have to do your best,

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:26.840
given what limited resources you have,

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:28.530
and what limited information you have,

00:11:28.530 --> 00:11:31.290
to make the best possible
and fairest decision.

00:11:31.290 --> 00:11:33.290
I applaud all first responders

00:11:33.290 --> 00:11:37.120
in these types of
mass-casualty situations.

00:11:37.120 --> 00:11:40.160
Growing up in New York
City, thinking about 9/11,

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:44.800
what that must have been like
for people who were working

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:46.740
in the local New York City hospitals,

00:11:46.740 --> 00:11:49.990
it scares me, it blows my
mind all at the same time.

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:53.210
I've trained in Level 1 trauma centers

00:11:53.210 --> 00:11:55.750
during my medical school time,

00:11:55.750 --> 00:11:59.230
and just having a few people
come in with gunshot wounds

00:11:59.230 --> 00:12:01.610
or knife wounds, or bicycle accidents,

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:04.520
those are incredibly stressful situations.

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:05.750
You never know what to expect.

00:12:05.750 --> 00:12:07.940
You always have to be
thinking quickly on your feet.

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:10.010
So then to imagine what it must be like

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:12.020
when hundreds of people or
even thousands of people

00:12:12.020 --> 00:12:13.810
are coming in, wow.

00:12:13.810 --> 00:12:15.710
So, huge thank you to all the people

00:12:15.710 --> 00:12:18.250
that have ever been involved
or may ever be involved

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:19.880
in those types of
situations in the future.

00:12:19.880 --> 00:12:22.640
We really respect you and
thank you for your service.

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:23.783
- Did she suffer?

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:26.950
- No.

00:12:26.950 --> 00:12:28.870
Her injuries...
- Again, these conversations

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:30.030
don't happen in the open.

00:12:30.030 --> 00:12:31.000
This is not right.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:32.810
- Better get cleaned up.

00:12:32.810 --> 00:12:35.183
Rounds in five minutes.

00:12:36.398 --> 00:12:39.990
♪ Today has been okay ♪

00:12:39.990 --> 00:12:40.823
- I don't get it.

00:12:40.823 --> 00:12:41.820
They work 30 hours (laughs)

00:12:41.820 --> 00:12:43.130
then they come in for an emergency,

00:12:43.130 --> 00:12:45.870
look like they worked
for another 12 hours,

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:47.070
they've been working for 42,

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:49.530
now they're going to morning rounds?

00:12:49.530 --> 00:12:51.080
Do they ever get time off?

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:53.740
Oh man, you guys recommended a doozy here.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:55.650
It presented an interesting situation,

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:58.150
unfortunate for the patient that died.

00:12:58.150 --> 00:12:59.960
Kind of shows you that
doctors are sometimes

00:12:59.960 --> 00:13:02.760
put into really tight spots
where they have to use

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:04.950
limited information, limited resources,

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:08.390
and sometimes let one life
go in order to save another.

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:12.140
That's a very, very, very
unfair and tight situation

00:13:12.140 --> 00:13:13.230
that we find ourselves in.

00:13:13.230 --> 00:13:15.500
That's what we asked for when
we decided to go into medicine

00:13:15.500 --> 00:13:16.920
and that's why it's a beautiful field

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:20.360
and we get so much admiration
put upon us as doctors,

00:13:20.360 --> 00:13:22.800
because we have to make those decisions.

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:24.470
I like Grey's Anatomy, it's a fun show.

00:13:24.470 --> 00:13:26.070
There's a lot of drama in it.

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:27.680
So if you have some more episodes,

00:13:27.680 --> 00:13:29.490
maybe ones that are more medical

00:13:29.490 --> 00:13:31.750
and less like, oh my god, McDreamy,

00:13:31.750 --> 00:13:33.370
does he love me, does he love me not?

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:35.930
Like I know that's important,
but I would love to comment

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:37.710
on some more medical aspects of the show.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:39.340
Drop it down below in the comments.

00:13:39.340 --> 00:13:42.470
Hit that Subscribe button
and the notification bell

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:44.910
so that you know when all
my videos are coming up.

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:46.915
As always, stay happy and healthy.

00:13:46.915 --> 00:13:47.748
(static hisses)

00:13:47.748 --> 00:13:50.240
That's why if you ever get
stabbed with something,

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:52.320
it's not ideal to just pull it out

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:54.256
until you have medical
care around you, because...

00:13:54.256 --> 00:13:57.130
(horn blares)

00:13:57.130 --> 00:13:58.353
What even was that?

